From: Robert August Vincent II (Bob) <pillarsdotnet@gmail.com>
Subject: Take zip 9 for its three advisories

zip 2.x, which upstream asks for, is affected by three advisories that
have no fix in the 2.x series:

 * CVE-2026-115905 (critical): ZipStreamReader extracts using local
   file header names while ZipArchive trusts the central directory, so
   a cloaked archive can write a path that inspection did not show.
   Fixed in 9.0.5.
 * CVE-2026-115909 (high): ZipArchive::extract reserves a symlink
   target from the archive's own uncompressed_size, which aborts the
   process on an impossible capacity. Fixed in 9.0.0.
 * CVE-2026-115906 (medium): the AES entry reader checks its HMAC only
   when the data counter reaches zero, so a truncated encrypted entry
   can return clean EOF unverified. Fixed in 9.0.0.

fnm reaches none of the three: it drives ZipArchive by index itself
rather than through ZipStreamReader or extract(), and reads no
encrypted entry. The dependency is updated regardless, so the package
carries no crate with a known advisory.

zip 9 returns a Result from name() and mangled_name(), both of which
decode what the archive claims, so the two call sites propagate the
error like every other failure in extract_into.

Forwarded: not-needed
